### When does HashMap resize?

HashMap maintains a `threshold` field equal to `capacity * loadFactor`. When `size` exceeds this threshold after a put operation, resize is triggered.

With default settings (capacity 16, load factor 0.75), the first resize happens when the 13th entry is added (threshold = 12). After resize, capacity becomes 32 and threshold becomes 24.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class ResizeTriggerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Capacity 16, load factor 0.75 → threshold 12
        Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>(16, 0.75f);

        for (int i = 1; i <= 15; i++) {
            map.put("ACC" + i, "Holder" + i);
            // Resize happens when 13th entry is added
            // (size > 12 after 12th entry triggers resize on 13th put)
        }

        System.out.println("Size: " + map.size());
        // Capacity is now 32 (doubled from 16)
        // Threshold is now 24 (32 * 0.75)
    }
}
```

```text
Size: 15
```

The growth sequence for default settings:

| Capacity | Threshold | Resize at entry |
|---|---|---|
| 16 | 12 | 13th |
| 32 | 24 | 25th |
| 64 | 48 | 49th |
| 128 | 96 | 97th |
| 256 | 192 | 193rd |
| 1024 | 768 | 769th |

### Why is capacity always a power of 2?

The bucket index is computed as `hash & (capacity - 1)`. When capacity is a power of 2, `capacity - 1` is a bitmask with all lower bits set:

- capacity 16 → mask 15 = `0b00001111`
- capacity 32 → mask 31 = `0b00011111`
- capacity 64 → mask 63 = `0b00111111`

Bitwise AND with this mask extracts the lower bits of the hash, which is equivalent to `hash % capacity`. But bitwise AND is a single CPU instruction, while modulo requires integer division – roughly 10x slower.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class BitwiseVsModuloDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int hash = "SAV2001".hashCode();
        int capacity = 16;

        // These produce the same result:
        int bitwiseIndex = hash & (capacity - 1); // fast: one instruction
        int moduloIndex = Math.floorMod(hash, capacity); // slow: division

        System.out.println("Bitwise: " + bitwiseIndex);
        System.out.println("Modulo:  " + moduloIndex);
        System.out.println("Same: " + (bitwiseIndex == moduloIndex));

        // Performance comparison
        int iterations = 100_000_000;
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            sum += i & 15; // bitwise
        }
        long bitwiseTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        start = System.nanoTime();
        sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            sum += i % 16; // modulo
        }
        long moduloTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.printf("Bitwise: %d ms%n", bitwiseTime / 1_000_000);
        System.out.printf("Modulo:  %d ms%n", moduloTime / 1_000_000);
    }
}
```

```text
Bitwise: 3
Modulo:  3
Same: true
Bitwise: 45 ms
Modulo:  128 ms
```

### How does Java 8 redistribute entries efficiently?

Before Java 8, every entry was rehashed with the new mask: `hash & (newCapacity - 1)`. This required recomputing the index for every entry.

Java 8 introduced an optimization. When capacity doubles from n to 2n, the new mask has one more bit than the old mask. For each entry, only that one extra bit matters:

- If the extra bit in the hash is 0, the entry stays at its current index.
- If the extra bit is 1, the entry moves to `oldIndex + oldCapacity`.

The check is simply `hash & oldCapacity`. This splits each bucket into exactly two groups with one bit check per entry – no full rehash needed.

```java
// Capacity 16 → 32
// Old mask: 0b01111 (15)
// New mask: 0b11111 (31)
// Extra bit: bit 4 (value 16 = oldCapacity)

// Entry with hash 0b...01011:
//   Old index: hash & 15 = 01011 & 01111 = 01011 = 11
//   Check:     hash & 16 = 01011 & 10000 = 00000 = 0 → STAYS at index 11

// Entry with hash 0b...11011:
//   Old index: hash & 15 = 11011 & 01111 = 01011 = 11
//   Check:     hash & 16 = 11011 & 10000 = 10000 ≠ 0 → MOVES to index 11 + 16 = 27
```

### What is the impact of load factor?

The load factor controls the trade-off between space and time:

| Load factor | Space | Collisions | Resize frequency |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.50 | 50% wasted | Very few | Frequent (more resizes) |
| 0.75 (default) | 25% wasted | Few | Balanced |
| 1.00 | 0% wasted | Many | Rare (more collisions) |
| 1.50 | Overpacked | Very many | Very rare |

The default 0.75 is well-studied and works for most applications. Lower values waste memory but give faster lookups. Higher values save memory but increase collision chains.

### How do you pre-size HashMap correctly?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class PreSizingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int expectedAccounts = 100_000;

        // Formula: expectedSize / loadFactor + 1
        int correctCapacity = (int) (expectedAccounts / 0.75) + 1; // 133334
        Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>(correctCapacity);
        // HashMap rounds up to next power of 2: 262144
        // Threshold = 262144 * 0.75 = 196608 > 100000 → no resize!

        // Simpler: expectedSize * 4/3 + 1 (integer math for 0.75)
        Map<String, BigDecimal> alt = new HashMap<>(expectedAccounts * 4 / 3 + 1);

        // Java 19+: factory method
        // Map<String, BigDecimal> modern = HashMap.newHashMap(expectedAccounts);

        // Load data without resizing
        for (int i = 0; i < expectedAccounts; i++) {
            balances.put("ACC" + String.format("%06d", i), BigDecimal.valueOf(i));
        }
        System.out.println("Loaded " + balances.size() + " accounts without resizing");
    }
}
```

```text
Loaded 100000 accounts without resizing
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Passing the expected size directly as capacity

```java
// WRONG: new HashMap<>(10000) with 10K entries
// Capacity 16384 (next power of 2), threshold 12288
// 10000 < 12288 → no resize. Happens to work, but wastes capacity.

// WRONG: new HashMap<>(1000) with 1000 entries
// Capacity 1024, threshold 768
// 1000 > 768 → resize at 769th entry!

// CORRECT: account for load factor
// new HashMap<>(1000 * 4 / 3 + 1) = new HashMap<>(1334)
// Capacity 2048, threshold 1536 → no resize for 1000 entries
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Resize cost.** For a HashMap with 10 million entries, resize copies 10 million entries to a new table. Each entry's bucket position may change. This is O(n) and can cause a noticeable latency spike.

- **Non-power-of-2 initial capacity.** If you pass a non-power-of-2 initial capacity (e.g., 1000), HashMap rounds up to the next power of 2 (1024). This is handled by `tableSizeFor()` which uses bit manipulation to find the next power of 2.

- **Maximum capacity.** HashMap's maximum capacity is `1 << 30` (1,073,741,824). Beyond this, the threshold is set to Integer.MAX_VALUE to prevent further resizing.

- **The hi/lo chain split.** During Java 8 resize, each bucket is split into two chains: `lo` (entries that stay) and `hi` (entries that move). These chains are built by iterating the original chain once, then the lo chain is placed at the old index and the hi chain at oldIndex + oldCapacity.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why is capacity a power of 2?" – Enables fast bitwise AND instead of modulo for bucket indexing.
- "What triggers a resize?" – size exceeding capacity * loadFactor.
- "How does Java 8 optimize resize?" – One-bit check (hash & oldCapacity) instead of full rehash.
- "How do you avoid resize?" – Pre-size with `new HashMap<>(expectedSize / 0.75 + 1)`.
- "What is the default load factor?" – 0.75.
